// 2019 GS1 (Prelims) Set A — Transcribed from "E:\UPSC question paper\2019\2019 prilims ques & answer.docx"
// Contains question, choices (A, B, C, D), answer, explanation, and upsc review points
export const paper = { year: 2019, name: 'GS1', set: 'A' };

export const questions = [
{"no":1,"text":"With reference to Mughal India, what is/are the difference/differences between Jagirdar and Zamindar?\n1. Jagirdars were holders of land assignments in lieu of judicial and police duties, whereas Zamindars were holders of revenue rights without obligation to perform any duty other than revenue collection.\n2. Land assignments to Jagirdars were hereditary and revenue rights of Zamindars were not hereditary.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Mughal Administration","Jagirdari System","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Jagirdars generally received revenue assignments in lieu of military/civil service, not specifically judicial or police duties.\nJagirs were generally not hereditary.\nZamindari rights, on the other hand, often became hereditary.\nTherefore, both statements are incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Jagirdar: Holder of a jagir/revenue assignment.\nJagirs were generally transferable and non-hereditary.\nZamindar: Had rights connected with land revenue collection and local authority.\nDistinguish jagir system from zamindari system."},
{"no":2,"text":"With reference to land reforms in independent India, which statement is correct?","A":"Ceiling laws were aimed at family holdings and not individual holdings.","B":"The major aim of land reforms was providing agricultural land to all the landless.","C":"It resulted in cultivation of cash crops as the predominant form of cultivation.","D":"Land reforms permitted no exemptions to ceiling limits.","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Land Reforms","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Land reforms sought to redistribute land and reduce inequalities in land ownership.\nA major objective was to provide land to landless agricultural workers.\nCeiling laws placed limits on the amount of land that could be held.\nCeiling laws could have exemptions under specific circumstances.","revisionPoints":"Major objectives: abolition of intermediaries, tenancy reforms, land ceilings and consolidation of holdings.\nLand reforms aimed at reducing concentration of land ownership.\nRedistribution was an important social and economic objective."},
{"no":3,"text":"The Global Competitiveness Report is published by","A":"IMF","B":"UNCTAD","C":"World Economic Forum","D":"World Bank","ans":"C","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Organizations","tags":["World Economic Forum","Global Competitiveness Report","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"The World Economic Forum (WEF) publishes the Global Competitiveness Report.\nIt evaluates factors related to the competitiveness of economies.","revisionPoints":"WEF → Global Competitiveness Report\nWorld Bank → World Development Report\nUNDP → Human Development Report\nIMF → World Economic Outlook"},
{"no":4,"text":"Consider the following statements about the Charter Act, 1813:\n1. It ended the East India Company's trade monopoly except tea and China trade.\n2. It asserted the sovereignty of the British Crown over Company territories.\n3. Revenues of India were now controlled by the British Parliament.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Charter Act 1813","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"The Charter Act of 1813 ended most of the East India Company's trade monopoly.\nThe Company retained monopoly over tea and trade with China.\nThe Act strengthened the position of the British Crown over Company territories.\nStatement 3 is incorrect according to the source.","revisionPoints":"Charter Act 1813: Ended most Company trade monopoly.\nExceptions: tea and China trade.\nAllowed Christian missionaries to operate in India.\nProvided for expenditure on education in India."},
{"no":5,"text":"Consider the following statements about the Swadeshi Movement:\n1. It revived indigenous crafts and industries.\n2. National Council of Education was established during it.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Swadeshi Movement","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"The Swadeshi Movement encouraged the use of indigenous goods.\nIt promoted indigenous industries and crafts.\nThe National Council of Education was established during the movement.","revisionPoints":"Swadeshi Movement began in 1905.\nClosely associated with opposition to the Partition of Bengal.\nPromoted boycott of foreign goods.\nEncouraged national education and indigenous industries."},
{"no":6,"text":"Match the organizations and leaders:\nAll India Anti-Untouchability League — Mahatma Gandhi\nAll India Kisan Sabha — Swami Sahajanand Saraswati\nSelf-Respect Movement — E.V. Ramaswami Naicker","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Freedom Struggle Organizations","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Mahatma Gandhi was associated with the All India Anti-Untouchability League.\nSwami Sahajanand Saraswati was a major leader of the All India Kisan Sabha.\nE.V. Ramaswami Naicker (Periyar) led the Self-Respect Movement.","revisionPoints":"Gandhi → Anti-Untouchability League\nSahajanand Saraswati → Kisan movement\nPeriyar → Self-Respect Movement\nSelf-Respect Movement was particularly influential in Tamil Nadu."},
{"no":7,"text":"Which one is not a Harappan site?","A":"Chanhudaro","B":"Kot Diji","C":"Sohgaura","D":"Desalpur","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Indus Valley Civilization","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Chanhudaro, Kot Diji and Desalpur are associated with the Harappan/Indus tradition.\nSohgaura is famous for the Sohgaura Copper Plate.\nTherefore, Sohgaura is the answer.","revisionPoints":"Chanhudaro: Harappan site.\nKot Diji: Early Harappan site in Sindh.\nDesalpur: Harappan site in Gujarat.\nSohgaura: Known for its ancient copper-plate inscription."},
{"no":8,"text":"\"Ranyo Ashoka\" inscription is found at","A":"Kanganahalli","B":"Sanchi","C":"Shahbazgarhi","D":"Sohgaura","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Ashoka","Kanganahalli","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"The Kanganahalli excavation yielded an inscription referring to Ashoka as \"Ranyo Ashoka\".\nIt is an important archaeological evidence associated with Ashoka.","revisionPoints":"Kanganahalli → \"Ranyo Ashoka\"\nKanganahalli is located in Karnataka.\nAshoka's inscriptions are important sources for understanding the Mauryan period."},
{"no":9,"text":"Which of the following are features of Mahayana Buddhism?\n1. Deification of Buddha\n2. Worship of Bodhisattvas\n3. Image worship and rituals","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Buddhism","Mahayana","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Mahayana Buddhism developed greater emphasis on Bodhisattvas.\nBuddha came to be represented through images and devotional practices.\nRituals and devotional elements became more prominent.","revisionPoints":"Mahayana → Bodhisattva ideal\nGreater emphasis on devotion and image worship.\nBuddha was increasingly represented in anthropomorphic form.\nTheravada places greater emphasis on the individual path toward liberation."},
{"no":10,"text":"Forced labour (Vishti) during the Gupta period","A":"It was considered a source of income for the State.","B":"It was absent in Madhya Pradesh and Kathiawar.","C":"Forced labourers were paid weekly wages.","D":"Eldest son alone performed forced labour.","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Gupta Period","Vishti","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Vishti refers to compulsory or forced labour.\nIt could be imposed by the state or authorities.\nIt was treated as a form of taxation/source of state income.","revisionPoints":"Vishti → Forced/compulsory labour\nImportant term for studying ancient Indian taxation and administration.\nGupta-period inscriptions provide evidence for various forms of obligations and revenue."},
{"no":11,"text":"\"Kalyana Mandapas\" became a notable feature during","A":"Chalukya","B":"Chandela","C":"Rashtrakuta","D":"Vijayanagara","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Vijayanagara Architecture","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Kalyana Mandapas were prominent architectural features of Vijayanagara temples.\nThey were often associated with ceremonial and ritual functions.","revisionPoints":"Kalyana Mandapa → Vijayanagara architecture\nVijayanagara temples are known for:\nLarge temple complexes\nMandapas\nTall gateways/gopurams\nPillared halls\nHampi is the major centre of Vijayanagara architecture."},
{"no":12,"text":"Consider the following statements about the Delhi Sultanate:\n1. Revenue collector was called Amil.\n2. Iqta system was indigenous.\n3. Mir Bakshi originated under Khaljis.","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Delhi Sultanate","Iqta System","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Amil was associated with revenue collection.\nThe Iqta system was not indigenous; it was borrowed from West Asian administrative traditions.\nMir Bakshi became prominent under the Mughals, not the Khaljis.","revisionPoints":"Amil → Revenue administration\nIqta → Revenue assignment\nIqta system had origins outside India.\nMir Bakshi → Mughal military/pay administration"},
{"no":13,"text":"Consider the following statements about saints:\n1. Nimbarka was contemporary of Akbar.\n2. Kabir was influenced by Shaikh Ahmad Sirhindi.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both","D":"Neither","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Bhakti Movement","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Nimbarka lived much earlier than Akbar.\nKabir also lived before Shaikh Ahmad Sirhindi.\nTherefore, neither statement is correct.","revisionPoints":"Kabir → Bhakti tradition\nNimbarka → Vaishnava tradition\nShaikh Ahmad Sirhindi → Naqshbandi Sufi tradition\nChronology is important when matching medieval personalities."},
{"no":14,"text":"Consider the following statements about British Colonial Rule:\n1. Gandhi helped abolish indentured labour.\n2. Gandhi opposed recruitment in the War Conference.\n3. Congress was declared illegal after Salt Satyagraha.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"1 and 3 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Gandhi","Freedom Struggle","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Gandhi campaigned against the indentured labour system.\nThe source states that Gandhi supported recruitment during World War I, making statement 2 incorrect.\nCongress was declared illegal during the repression following the Civil Disobedience/Salt Satyagraha phase.","revisionPoints":"Indentured labour: Important issue in the Indian diaspora and nationalist movement.\nGandhi worked against the indentured labour system.\nGandhi's position during World War I should be studied separately from his later anti-war/anti-colonial positions.\nSalt Satyagraha began in 1930."},
{"no":15,"text":"Which of the following are correctly matched?\n1. Tej Bahadur Sapru – President, All India Liberal Federation\n2. K.C. Neogy – Constituent Assembly Member\n3. P.C. Joshi – General Secretary, Communist Party of India","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Freedom Struggle Personalities","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Tej Bahadur Sapru was associated with the All India Liberal Federation.\nK.C. Neogy was a member of the Constituent Assembly.\nP.C. Joshi served as General Secretary of the Communist Party of India.","revisionPoints":"Tej Bahadur Sapru → Liberal politics\nK.C. Neogy → Constituent Assembly\nP.C. Joshi → Communist Party of India\nUPSC frequently tests person–organization–position combinations."},
{"no":16,"text":"With reference to Mian Tansen, which statement is NOT correct?","A":"\"Tansen\" title was given by Akbar.","B":"He composed Dhrupads on Hindu gods.","C":"He composed songs praising patrons.","D":"He invented many ragas.","ans":"D","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Music","tags":["Tansen","Mughal Court Music","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Tansen was one of the most celebrated musicians associated with Akbar's court.\nHe composed Dhrupad compositions.\nHe composed works praising patrons.\nThe source distinguishes developing/popularizing ragas from inventing them.","revisionPoints":"Tansen → Akbar's court\nAssociated with Dhrupad tradition.\nFamous for musical compositions and development of ragas.\nPart of Akbar's celebrated Navratnas tradition."},
{"no":17,"text":"Which Mughal emperor shifted emphasis from illustrated manuscripts to portraits?","A":"Humayun","B":"Akbar","C":"Jahangir","D":"Shah Jahan","ans":"C","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Painting","tags":["Mughal Painting","Jahangir","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Mughal painting developed significantly under Akbar.\nUnder Jahangir, there was greater emphasis on portraiture and naturalistic representation.\nJahangir himself had strong interest in painting.","revisionPoints":"Akbar → Expansion of illustrated manuscripts\nJahangir → Portraiture and naturalism\nMughal painting incorporated Persian, Indian and European influences.\nShah Jahan → Greater emphasis on courtly elegance and decorative style"},
{"no":18,"text":"Which National Park lies completely in the temperate alpine zone?","A":"Manas","B":"Namdapha","C":"Neora Valley","D":"Valley of Flowers","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Valley of Flowers","National Parks","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Valley of Flowers National Park is situated in the high Himalayan region of Uttarakhand.\nIt is characterized by alpine meadows and high-altitude Himalayan vegetation.","revisionPoints":"Valley of Flowers → Uttarakhand\nLocated in the Western Himalayas.\nFamous for alpine flowering plants.\nPart of the Nanda Devi and Valley of Flowers UNESCO World Heritage Site."},
{"no":19,"text":"Atal Innovation Mission is under","A":"Department of Science & Technology","B":"Ministry of Labour","C":"NITI Aayog","D":"Ministry of Skill Development","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["Atal Innovation Mission","NITI Aayog","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Atal Innovation Mission (AIM) is a flagship initiative of NITI Aayog.\nIt promotes innovation and entrepreneurship.","revisionPoints":"AIM → NITI Aayog\nFocus areas include:\nInnovation\nEntrepreneurship\nIncubation\nSchool-level innovation\nAtal Tinkering Labs are associated with AIM."},
{"no":20,"text":"On 21 June, the Sun","A":"Does not set below the horizon at the Arctic Circle.","B":"Does not set below the horizon at the Antarctic Circle.","C":"Is overhead at the Equator.","D":"Is overhead at the Tropic of Capricorn.","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Solstice","Arctic Circle","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"21 June is the Northern Hemisphere's summer solstice.\nThe Sun's vertical rays are over the Tropic of Cancer.\nThe Arctic region experiences continuous daylight around the summer solstice.\nThe Antarctic region experiences the opposite phenomenon—polar night.","revisionPoints":"21 June → Summer Solstice in Northern Hemisphere\nSun overhead at Tropic of Cancer.\nArctic Circle → Midnight Sun\nAntarctic Circle → Polar night\n22/23 September → Equinox\n21/22 December → Winter Solstice in Northern Hemisphere"},
{"no":21,"text":"Which one of the following groups of plants was domesticated in the New World and introduced into the Old World?","A":"Tobacco, cocoa and rubber","B":"Tobacco, cotton and rubber","C":"Cotton, coffee and sugarcane","D":"Rubber, coffee and wheat","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Economic Geography","tags":["Crop Origins","New World Crops","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Tobacco, cocoa and rubber originated in the Americas.\nThey were subsequently introduced to the Old World.\nCoffee originated in Ethiopia.\nWheat originated in West Asia.\nCotton has both Old World and New World varieties.","revisionPoints":"New World → Americas\nOld World → Europe, Asia and Africa\nTobacco → Americas\nCocoa → Americas\nRubber → Americas\nCoffee → Ethiopia; Wheat → West Asia"},
{"no":22,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Asiatic lions are naturally found only in India.\n2. Double-humped camel is naturally found only in India.\n3. One-horned rhinoceros is naturally found only in India.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Asiatic Lion","Endemic Species","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"The Asiatic lion survives naturally only in India, primarily in the Gir landscape of Gujarat.\nThe double-humped camel is also naturally found outside India, particularly in parts of Central Asia.\nThe one-horned rhinoceros is also naturally found in Nepal.","revisionPoints":"Asiatic Lion → Gir, Gujarat\nOne-horned Rhinoceros → India and Nepal\nDouble-humped/Bactrian Camel → Central Asia\nDistribution-based questions require distinguishing endemic from naturally occurring in multiple countries."},
{"no":23,"text":"Match the famous places with rivers:\nPandharpur — Chandrabhaga\nTiruchirappalli — Cauvery\nHampi — Malaprabha","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Rivers of India","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Pandharpur → Chandrabhaga River.\nTiruchirappalli → Cauvery River.\nHampi is situated on the Tungabhadra, not the Malaprabha.","revisionPoints":"Pandharpur → Chandrabhaga\nTiruchirappalli → Cauvery\nHampi → Tungabhadra\nRemember important city/place–river associations."},
{"no":24,"text":"Official poverty lines differ among States because","A":"Poverty rates differ","B":"Price levels differ","C":"Gross State Product differs","D":"Public Distribution System differs","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Poverty Line","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Poverty lines are connected with the cost of living.\nPrice levels vary across States.\nTherefore, the monetary value required to purchase a comparable basket can differ.","revisionPoints":"Poverty line is related to consumption expenditure.\nPrice differences → different monetary poverty thresholds.\nDo not confuse poverty line with the actual poverty ratio.\nPoverty measurement methodology has changed over time in India."},
{"no":25,"text":"Cirrus cloud thinning and sulphate aerosol injection into the stratosphere are suggested for","A":"Artificial rain","B":"Cyclone reduction","C":"Solar wind reduction","D":"Reducing global warming","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["Geoengineering","Solar Radiation Management","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"These are proposed forms of climate geoengineering.\nStratospheric aerosol injection seeks to reflect a portion of incoming solar radiation.\nCirrus cloud thinning seeks to reduce the greenhouse effect associated with high-altitude cirrus clouds.","revisionPoints":"Geoengineering → deliberate intervention in Earth's climate system\nStratospheric aerosol injection → increases reflection of solar radiation.\nThese are proposed techniques, not conventional climate-mitigation methods.\nPotential environmental risks and unintended consequences are major concerns."},
{"no":26,"text":"\"Pyrolysis\" and \"Plasma Gasification\" are related to","A":"Rare earth extraction","B":"Natural gas extraction","C":"Hydrogen fuel vehicles","D":"Waste-to-energy technologies","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Environmental Technology","tags":["Waste-to-Energy","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Pyrolysis thermally decomposes material in limited or absent oxygen.\nPlasma gasification uses very high temperatures to convert waste into syngas and other products.\nBoth can be used in waste-to-energy systems.","revisionPoints":"Pyrolysis → thermal decomposition with little/no oxygen\nGasification → conversion into combustible synthesis gas\nPlasma gasification uses extremely high temperatures.\nApplications include municipal and industrial waste management."},
{"no":27,"text":"Which of the following are in Agasthyamalai Biosphere Reserve?","A":"Neyyar, Peppara, Shendurney WLS; Kalakad-Mundanthurai Tiger Reserve","B":"Mudumalai, Sathyamangalam, Wayanad, Silent Valley","C":"Kaundinya, Gundla Brahmeswaram, Papikonda, Mukurthi","D":"Kawal, Sri Venkateswara, Nagarjunasagar-Srisailam","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Biosphere Reserve","Agasthyamalai","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"The Agasthyamalai Biosphere Reserve extends across Kerala and Tamil Nadu.\nThe source associates it with Neyyar, Peppara, Shendurney and Kalakad-Mundanthurai Tiger Reserve.\nThe other groups contain protected areas belonging to different parts of the country.","revisionPoints":"Agasthyamalai → Western Ghats\nSpans Kerala and Tamil Nadu.\nImportant protected areas include:\nNeyyar\nPeppara\nShendurney\nKalakad-Mundanthurai\nBiosphere reserves can include multiple national parks, sanctuaries and other ecosystems."},
{"no":28,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Some turtles are herbivores.\n2. Some fish are herbivores.\n3. Some marine mammals are herbivores.\n4. Some snakes are viviparous.","A":"1 and 3 only","B":"2, 3 and 4 only","C":"2 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Marine Biology","Reptiles","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Green sea turtles can be herbivorous, particularly as adults.\nSome fish, such as grass carp, feed mainly on vegetation.\nDugongs and manatees are herbivorous marine mammals.\nSeveral snake species give birth to live young and are therefore viviparous.","revisionPoints":"Green sea turtle → herbivorous adult diet.\nGrass carp → herbivorous fish.\nDugong/manatee → herbivorous marine mammals.\nSome snakes → viviparous or ovoviviparous reproduction."},
{"no":29,"text":"Which of the following wildlife are naturally found in the indicated locations?\n1. Blue-finned Mahseer — Cauvery\n2. Irrawaddy Dolphin — Chambal\n3. Rusty-spotted Cat — Eastern Ghats","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Wildlife","Rivers","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Blue-finned Mahseer occurs in the Cauvery system.\nThe source states that the Chambal is associated with the Gangetic Dolphin, not the Irrawaddy Dolphin.\nRusty-spotted Cat occurs in parts of the Eastern Ghats.","revisionPoints":"Blue-finned Mahseer → Cauvery\nGangetic Dolphin → Chambal\nRusty-spotted Cat → parts of peninsular India\nUPSC often tests species through their geographical distribution."},
{"no":30,"text":"Why is there concern about microbeads?","A":"They harm marine ecosystems.","B":"They cause skin cancer.","C":"They are absorbed by crops.","D":"They are used as food adulterants.","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Pollution","tags":["Microbeads","Marine Pollution","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Microbeads are very small plastic particles.\nThey can enter aquatic ecosystems through wastewater.\nMarine organisms may ingest them.\nThey contribute to plastic pollution and can enter food chains.","revisionPoints":"Microbeads → tiny plastic particles\nMajor concern → marine and freshwater pollution\nCan be ingested by aquatic organisms.\nAssociated with the broader problem of microplastics."},
{"no":31,"text":"Himalayan nettle (Girardinia diversifolia) is valued because it is a source of","A":"Anti-malarial drug","B":"Biodiesel","C":"Paper pulp","D":"Textile fibre","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Himalayan Nettle","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Himalayan nettle contains strong natural fibres.\nThese fibres can be processed and used for textile production.\nIt is therefore significant as a traditional and natural fibre resource.","revisionPoints":"Girardinia diversifolia → Himalayan nettle.\nImportant source of natural fibre.\nNatural fibres can have applications in textiles and handicrafts.\nBiodiversity questions may connect species with their economic uses."},
{"no":32,"text":"Satellite remote sensing can estimate:\n1. Chlorophyll content\n2. Greenhouse gas emissions from paddy fields\n3. Land surface temperature","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Space","tags":["Remote Sensing","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Remote sensing uses electromagnetic radiation detected by satellites.\nVegetation characteristics such as chlorophyll can be estimated through spectral information.\nSatellite-based methods can help estimate land surface temperature.\nAdvanced remote sensing can also contribute to monitoring greenhouse gas emissions.","revisionPoints":"Remote sensing → collection of information without direct physical contact.\nChlorophyll → vegetation spectral characteristics\nLand Surface Temperature → thermal remote sensing\nSatellites can support agriculture, climate and environmental monitoring."},
{"no":33,"text":"Arrange the following States in ascending order of forest cover as a percentage of geographical area:\n1. Chhattisgarh\n2. Madhya Pradesh\n3. Maharashtra\n4. Odisha","A":"2-3-1-4","B":"2-3-4-1","C":"3-2-4-1","D":"3-2-1-4","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Economic Geography","tags":["Forest Cover","India Geography","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"According to the source, the ascending order is:\nMadhya Pradesh\nMaharashtra\nChhattisgarh\nOdisha","revisionPoints":"This question tests forest cover as a percentage of geographical area, not total forest area.\nAlways distinguish:\nAbsolute forest area\nPercentage forest cover\nState-wise forest data are periodically reported through the India State of Forest Report."},
{"no":34,"text":"Consider the following statements about Methane Hydrate:\n1. Global warming may release methane.\n2. Large deposits exist beneath Arctic tundra and seafloor.\n3. Atmospheric methane oxidizes to CO₂ after about a decade.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["Methane Hydrate","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Methane hydrates contain methane trapped within ice-like structures.\nLarge deposits occur in marine sediments and permafrost regions.\nWarming can destabilize some methane hydrate deposits.\nAtmospheric methane has a relatively short atmospheric lifetime compared with CO₂ and is eventually oxidized, producing CO₂ and water.","revisionPoints":"Methane hydrate → methane trapped in crystalline water structures\nMajor locations:\nSeafloor sediments\nPermafrost regions\nMethane is a powerful greenhouse gas.\nMethane has a shorter atmospheric lifetime than CO₂ but greater warming potency per molecule over relevant time horizons."},
{"no":35,"text":"Which gases are released during crop residue burning?\n1. Carbon monoxide\n2. Methane\n3. Ozone\n4. Sulphur dioxide","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2, 3 and 4 only","C":"1 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Pollution","tags":["Crop Burning","Air Pollution","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Crop-residue burning releases several air pollutants.\nCarbon monoxide (CO) is directly emitted.\nMethane can also be released through incomplete combustion.\nSulphur-containing material can contribute to SO₂ emissions.\nBurning also contributes to formation of ground-level ozone through precursor emissions.","revisionPoints":"Crop burning → CO, particulate matter, NOx and other pollutants.\nContributes to regional air pollution.\nEmissions can contribute to tropospheric ozone formation.\nAlso releases greenhouse gases."},
{"no":36,"text":"Match the Sea with the bordering country:\nAdriatic Sea — Albania\nBlack Sea — Croatia\nCaspian Sea — Kazakhstan\nMediterranean Sea — Morocco\nRed Sea — Syria","A":"1, 2, 4","B":"1, 3, 4","C":"2, 5","D":"1, 2, 3, 4, 5","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Seas","World Geography","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Adriatic Sea → Albania ✔\nCaspian Sea → Kazakhstan ✔\nMediterranean Sea → Morocco ✔\nCroatia does not border the Black Sea.\nSyria does not border the Red Sea.","revisionPoints":"Adriatic Sea → Albania, Croatia, Italy, Montenegro, Slovenia, Bosnia and Herzegovina.\nCaspian Sea → Russia, Kazakhstan, Turkmenistan, Iran, Azerbaijan.\nMediterranean Sea → North Africa and Southern Europe.\nRed Sea → between northeastern Africa and Arabian Peninsula."},
{"no":37,"text":"Which country was the largest exporter of rice during the last five years?","A":"China","B":"India","C":"Myanmar","D":"Vietnam","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Economic Geography","tags":["Rice Exports","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"According to the source, India remained the world's largest rice exporter during the period considered.\nIndia's major rice exports include both basmati and non-basmati varieties.","revisionPoints":"India is a major global producer and exporter of rice.\nImportant rice-exporting countries include:\nIndia\nThailand\nVietnam\nPakistan\nDistinguish largest producer from largest exporter."},
{"no":38,"text":"Match the Glacier with the River:\nBandarpunch — Yamuna\nBara Shigri — Chenab\nMilam — Mandakini\nSiachen — Nubra\nZemu — Manas","A":"1, 2, 4","B":"1, 3, 4","C":"2, 5","D":"3, 5","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Glaciers","Rivers of India","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Bandarpunch → Yamuna ✔\nBara Shigri → Chenab ✔\nSiachen → Nubra ✔\nMilam feeds the Gori Ganga, not Mandakini.\nZemu feeds the Teesta, not Manas.","revisionPoints":"Bandarpunch → Yamuna system.\nBara Shigri → Chenab system.\nMilam → Gori Ganga.\nSiachen → Nubra/Shyok system.\nZemu → Teesta system."},
{"no":39,"text":"Carbofuran, methyl parathion, phorate and triazophos are","A":"Pesticides","B":"Food preservatives","C":"Fruit ripening agents","D":"Cosmetic chemicals","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Pollution","tags":["Pesticides","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"These are chemical compounds used as pesticides.\nSeveral of them are highly toxic and have environmental and health concerns.\nTheir use and regulation are therefore important from an environmental perspective.","revisionPoints":"Carbofuran → pesticide\nMethyl parathion → organophosphorus pesticide\nPhorate → organophosphorus pesticide\nTriazophos → organophosphorus pesticide\nPesticide exposure can affect humans, animals and ecosystems."},
{"no":40,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding the Wetlands (Conservation and Management) Rules:\n1. Ramsar Convention mandates India to protect all wetlands.\n2. Wetlands Rules, 2010 were framed considering Ramsar principles.\n3. Wetlands Rules include drainage/catchment area of wetlands.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Water Resources","tags":["Wetlands Rules","Ramsar Convention","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"The Ramsar Convention does not require every wetland in India to be protected.\nThe source marks statement 2 as incorrect.\nStatement 3 is considered correct in the source because the rules take account of the drainage/catchment area associated with wetlands.","revisionPoints":"Ramsar Convention → wetlands of international importance.\nRamsar does not mean every wetland automatically becomes a Ramsar Site.\nWetland conservation involves:\nWater regime\nEcological character\nCatchment/drainage influences\nIndia is a contracting party to the Ramsar Convention."},
{"no":41,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Agricultural soils release nitrogen oxides into the environment.\n2. Cattle release ammonia into the environment.\n3. Poultry industry releases reactive nitrogen compounds into the environment.","A":"1 and 3 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"2 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Pollution","tags":["Nitrogen Emissions","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Agricultural soils can emit nitrous oxide (N₂O).\nCattle release ammonia, mainly through manure and urine.\nPoultry farming also contributes reactive nitrogen emissions, especially ammonia.","revisionPoints":"Agricultural soils → N₂O emissions\nLivestock manure → ammonia\nPoultry farming → reactive nitrogen compounds.\nExcess reactive nitrogen can contribute to eutrophication, air pollution and climate change."},
{"no":42,"text":"What is common to Aliyar, Isapur and Kangsabati?","A":"Uranium deposits","B":"Tropical rain forests","C":"Underground cave systems","D":"Water reservoirs","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Dams","Reservoirs","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Aliyar, Isapur and Kangsabati are associated with water storage reservoirs/dams.\nThey are part of India's irrigation and water-management infrastructure.","revisionPoints":"Aliyar → Tamil Nadu\nIsapur → Maharashtra\nKangsabati → West Bengal\nReservoirs support irrigation, water supply and related uses."},
{"no":43,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding Hydrogen-enriched CNG (H-CNG):\n1. It eliminates carbon monoxide emissions.\n2. It reduces carbon dioxide and hydrocarbon emissions.\n3. Up to one-fifth hydrogen can be blended with CNG.\n4. It makes fuel less expensive than CNG.","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Renewable Energy","tags":["H-CNG","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"H-CNG is a mixture of hydrogen and compressed natural gas.\nIt can reduce certain emissions but does not eliminate carbon monoxide completely.\nHydrogen blending can be around 18–20% in the context given by the source.\nH-CNG is not necessarily cheaper than conventional CNG.","revisionPoints":"H-CNG → Hydrogen + CNG\nHydrogen can improve combustion characteristics.\nIt can reduce some pollutant emissions.\nDo not confuse H-CNG with pure hydrogen fuel."},
{"no":44,"text":"Why are dew drops not formed on cloudy nights?","A":"Clouds absorb Earth's outgoing radiation.","B":"Clouds reflect Earth's radiation.","C":"Earth's surface remains warmer on cloudy nights.","D":"Clouds deflect winds.","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Dew Formation","Radiation","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"At night, the Earth's surface loses heat through terrestrial radiation.\nClouds reduce the rate at which this heat escapes.\nTherefore, the surface remains relatively warmer.\nIt may not cool enough to reach the dew point, preventing dew formation.","revisionPoints":"Clear nights → greater radiational cooling.\nCloudy nights → reduced net radiational cooling.\nDew forms when a surface cools to its dew point.\nCloud cover can therefore reduce dew formation."},
{"no":45,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. The 44th Constitutional Amendment inserted Article 329A placing the election of the Prime Minister beyond judicial review.\n2. Supreme Court struck down the 99th Constitutional Amendment as violating judicial independence.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitutional Amendment","tags":["NJAC","Constitutional Amendment","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Article 329A was inserted by the 39th Constitutional Amendment, not the 44th.\nThe 39th Amendment dealt with disputes relating to elections of certain high constitutional offices.\nThe Supreme Court struck down the 99th Constitutional Amendment, which created the NJAC, in 2015.","revisionPoints":"39th Amendment → Article 329A\n44th Amendment → major restoration/reversal of Emergency-era changes\n99th Amendment → NJAC\nNJAC was struck down by the Supreme Court in 2015."},
{"no":46,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding impeachment/removal of Supreme Court Judges:\n1. Motion cannot be rejected by the Speaker.\n2. Constitution defines incapacity and proved misbehaviour.\n3. Procedure is provided under the Judges (Inquiry) Act, 1968.\n4. Removal requires special majority in both Houses.","A":"1 and 2","B":"3 only","C":"3 and 4 only","D":"1, 3 and 4","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Judiciary","tags":["Judges Impeachment","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"The Speaker/Chairman can admit or reject a removal motion according to the prescribed procedure.\nThe Constitution does not precisely define the terms \"proved misbehaviour\" and \"incapacity\".\nThe detailed procedure is provided by the Judges (Inquiry) Act, 1968.\nRemoval requires the prescribed special majority in both Houses of Parliament.","revisionPoints":"Judges of Supreme Court can be removed only through a Parliamentary process.\nGrounds: proved misbehaviour or incapacity.\nBoth Houses must pass the motion with the required special majority.\nPresident issues the order of removal after the parliamentary address."},
{"no":47,"text":"The Ninth Schedule was introduced during whose Prime Ministership?","A":"Jawaharlal Nehru","B":"Lal Bahadur Shastri","C":"Indira Gandhi","D":"Morarji Desai","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Ninth Schedule","First Amendment","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"The Ninth Schedule was introduced through the First Constitutional Amendment Act, 1951.\nAt that time, Jawaharlal Nehru was Prime Minister.","revisionPoints":"First Amendment, 1951 → Ninth Schedule\nInitially connected with protection of certain land reform laws.\nLaws in the Ninth Schedule are not completely immune from judicial review after later constitutional jurisprudence.\nImportant case: I.R. Coelho v. State of Tamil Nadu (2007)."},
{"no":48,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding the coal sector:\n1. Coal sector was nationalized under Indira Gandhi.\n2. Coal blocks are now allocated by lottery.\n3. India is now self-sufficient in coal.","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Mining & Minerals","tags":["Coal Sector","Nationalization","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Coal nationalization took place during Indira Gandhi's government.\nCoal blocks are allocated through mechanisms including auction, not lottery.\nIndia has continued to import coal, so the third statement is incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Coal nationalization occurred in the 1970s.\nCoal India Limited is a major public-sector coal producer.\nCoal block allocation has increasingly involved competitive auction.\nIndia remains a major coal producer as well as an importer."},
{"no":49,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding the Parliament (Prevention of Disqualification) Act, 1959:\n1. It exempts several posts from Office of Profit disqualification.\n2. It has been amended five times.\n3. Office of Profit is well defined in the Constitution.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"3 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["Office of Profit","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"The Act specifies offices that are exempted from disqualification on the ground of holding an office of profit.\nThe source states that it has been amended several times, including five amendments in the context of the question.\nThe Constitution does not provide a precise definition of \"Office of Profit\".","revisionPoints":"Articles 102 and 191 → Office of Profit\nOffice of Profit can lead to disqualification from Parliament/State Legislature.\nParliament can exempt specified offices through legislation.\nExact application depends on constitutional and statutory provisions."},
{"no":50,"text":"Transfer of tribal land to private parties for mining can be declared void under","A":"Third Schedule","B":"Fifth Schedule","C":"Ninth Schedule","D":"Twelfth Schedule","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Fifth Schedule","Tribal Land","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"The Fifth Schedule deals with administration and control of Scheduled Areas and Scheduled Tribes.\nIt provides a constitutional framework for protecting tribal interests in Scheduled Areas.\nRegulations can restrict or prohibit transfer of tribal land in such areas.","revisionPoints":"Fifth Schedule → Scheduled Areas and Scheduled Tribes\nApplies to specified areas in several States.\nSixth Schedule → Tribal areas in Assam, Meghalaya, Tripura and Mizoram\nGovernor has special responsibilities under the Fifth Schedule."},
{"no":51,"text":"Consider the following statements about Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Groups (PVTGs):\n1. They are found in 18 States and 1 Union Territory.\n2. Declining population is a criterion.\n3. 95 PVTGs are notified.\n4. Irular and Konda Reddi are PVTGs.","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"2, 3 and 4","C":"1, 2 and 4","D":"1, 3 and 4","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Tribal Welfare","tags":["PVTG","Tribal Welfare","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"The source states that PVTGs are found in 18 States and 1 Union Territory.\nDeclining or stagnant population is one of the criteria.\nThe number is 75, not 95, according to the source.\nIrular and Konda Reddi are included among PVTGs.","revisionPoints":"PVTG = Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Group.\nCriteria historically include:\nPre-agricultural level of technology\nLow literacy\nEconomic backwardness\nDeclining/stagnant population\nSource figure: 75 PVTGs."},
{"no":52,"text":"Ordinary laws cannot limit Article 142 because","A":"Election Commission decisions cannot be challenged.","B":"Supreme Court is not constrained by ordinary laws while exercising Article 142.","C":"President can declare Financial Emergency without Cabinet advice.","D":"States cannot legislate without Union concurrence.","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Judiciary","tags":["Article 142","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Article 142 gives the Supreme Court power to pass orders necessary for doing complete justice.\nThe source emphasizes that ordinary legislation cannot automatically restrict the exercise of this constitutional power.","revisionPoints":"Article 142 → Complete justice\nApplies to the Supreme Court of India.\nIt is a constitutional power.\nIt is frequently discussed in cases involving extraordinary remedies."},
{"no":53,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding the Legislative Assembly of a State:\n1. Governor addresses the first session every year.\n2. State Legislature follows Lok Sabha Rules if it has no rule.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both","D":"Neither","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Governor","tags":["Governor's Address","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"The Governor addresses the State Legislature at the first session after each general election and at the first session of each year.\nState Legislatures frame and operate under their own rules of procedure.\nThey do not automatically follow Lok Sabha rules merely because they lack a particular provision.","revisionPoints":"Article 176 → Governor's special address\nFirst session after general election → address.\nFirst session of every year → address.\nState Legislature has its own rules of procedure and conduct of business."},
{"no":54,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding the United Nations Convention against Corruption (UNCAC):\n1. It has a Protocol against Smuggling of Migrants.\n2. It is the first legally binding global anti-corruption instrument.\n3. UNTOC contains a chapter on asset recovery.\n4. UNODC assists implementation.","A":"1 and 3 only","B":"2, 3 and 4 only","C":"2 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"C","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Organizations","tags":["UNCAC","Anti-Corruption","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"The Protocol against the Smuggling of Migrants belongs to the UN Convention against Transnational Organized Crime (UNTOC).\nUNCAC is the first global legally binding anti-corruption instrument.\nAsset recovery is an important chapter of UNCAC, not UNTOC.\nUNODC supports implementation of UNCAC.","revisionPoints":"UNCAC → Anti-corruption\nUNTOC → Transnational organized crime\nUNODC → United Nations Office on Drugs and Crime.\nUNCAC covers:\nPrevention\nCriminalization\nInternational cooperation\nAsset recovery"},
{"no":55,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding the Forest Rights Act and Bamboo:\n1. Forest dwellers can cut bamboo due to amendment of the Indian Forest Act.\n2. Bamboo is a minor forest produce.\n3. Forest dwellers have ownership rights over minor forest produce.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Sustainable Development","tags":["Forest Rights Act","Minor Forest Produce","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Bamboo is classified as a Minor Forest Produce (MFP) in the context of the Forest Rights Act.\nEligible forest-dwelling communities can have ownership rights over MFP.\nThe source attributes these rights to the Forest Rights Act, rather than the Indian Forest Act amendment.","revisionPoints":"Forest Rights Act, 2006\nMFP includes products collected from forests other than timber.\nRights over MFP are important for tribal livelihoods.\nBamboo has significant economic and livelihood value."},
{"no":56,"text":"Which Article safeguards one's right to marry a person of one's choice?","A":"Article 19","B":"Article 21","C":"Article 25","D":"Article 29","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Fundamental Rights","tags":["Article 21","Right to Marry","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"The right to marry a person of one's choice has been recognized by the Supreme Court as part of personal liberty.\nArticle 21 protects the right to life and personal liberty.\nThe choice of spouse is connected with individual autonomy and dignity.","revisionPoints":"Article 21 → Right to life and personal liberty\nIncludes protection of personal autonomy and dignity.\nRight to choose one's spouse has been recognized through judicial interpretation.\nFundamental Rights are enforceable through constitutional remedies."},
{"no":57,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. A biological process to create a seed can be patented in India.\n2. India has no Intellectual Property Appellate Board (IPAB).\n3. Plant varieties are not eligible for patents in India.","A":"1 and 3 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Intellectual Property","tags":["Patents","IPAB","PPV&FR Act","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Biological processes are generally excluded from patentability under Indian patent law.\nThe source notes that the IPAB existed in 2019 and was abolished later, in 2021.\nPlant varieties are protected through the Protection of Plant Varieties and Farmers' Rights Act, rather than ordinary patent protection.","revisionPoints":"PPV&FR Act, 2001 → Plant variety protection\nBiological processes are subject to patentability exclusions.\nIPAB → abolished in 2021.\nIntellectual property includes patents, trademarks, copyrights and geographical indications."},
{"no":58,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986:\n1. Government may prescribe public participation procedures.\n2. Government may prescribe standards for emission/discharge of pollutants.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Environmental Law","tags":["Environment Protection Act","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"The Environment (Protection) Act gives broad powers to the Central Government for environmental protection.\nIt enables prescription of standards for emissions and discharge of pollutants.\nIt also provides scope for procedures and safeguards connected with environmental regulation.","revisionPoints":"Environment (Protection) Act → 1986\nEnacted after the Bhopal Gas Tragedy.\nProvides broad powers to the Central Government.\nImportant framework legislation for environmental regulation in India."},
{"no":59,"text":"Solid Waste Management Rules, 2016 — Which statement is correct?","A":"Waste generators must segregate waste into five categories.","B":"Applicable only to notified urban areas.","C":"Rules provide exact criteria for landfill sites.","D":"Waste cannot be transported outside a district.","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Pollution","tags":["Solid Waste Management Rules","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"According to the source, the categories include:\nBiodegradable waste\nDry waste\nDomestic hazardous waste\nSanitary waste\nHorticulture/garden waste, where applicable","revisionPoints":"Solid Waste Management Rules → 2016\nSource segregation is a key principle.\nWaste management follows a hierarchy emphasizing:\nReduction\nReuse\nRecycling\nRecovery\nScientific disposal\nLocal bodies have important responsibilities in implementation."},
{"no":60,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding the Industrial Employment (Standing Orders) Rules, 2018:\n1. Fixed-term employment makes laying off easier.\n2. No termination notice is necessary for fixed-term employees after contract expiry.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both","D":"Neither","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Labour & Employment","tags":["Fixed-term Employment","Labour Laws","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Fixed-term employment means employment is for a specified period.\nWhen the agreed contract period expires, the employment ends according to the terms of the contract.\nThis is distinct from an employer laying off a continuing employee.","revisionPoints":"Fixed-term employment → predetermined duration\nExpiry of contract is different from retrenchment/layoff.\nStanding Orders deal with conditions of employment and workplace rules.\nLabour-law questions often test distinctions between termination, retrenchment, layoff and contract expiry."},
{"no":61,"text":"Service Area Approach was implemented under","A":"IRDP","B":"Lead Bank Scheme","C":"MGNREGA","D":"NSDM","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking","tags":["Lead Bank Scheme","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"The Service Area Approach was introduced under the Lead Bank Scheme.\nIt aimed to improve banking services and credit delivery in rural areas.\nBank branches were assigned specific geographical areas for focused development.","revisionPoints":"Lead Bank Scheme → 1969\nService Area Approach → rural credit delivery.\nFocus → coordination between banks and local development agencies.\nImportant for financial inclusion and rural development."},
{"no":62,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding Minor Minerals:\n1. Sand is a minor mineral.\n2. States grant leases, but Centre frames rules.\n3. States frame rules against illegal mining.","A":"1 and 3 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Mining & Minerals","tags":["Minor Minerals","Sand Mining","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Sand is classified as a minor mineral.\nStates have powers relating to the administration and regulation of minor minerals.\nStates can frame rules to prevent illegal mining.\nThe source considers statement 2 incorrect because the relevant rule-making framework is substantially under the States.","revisionPoints":"Minor minerals → largely regulated by State Governments\nSand is an important minor mineral.\nMajor minerals have a greater role for the Central Government under the Mines and Minerals framework.\nIllegal mining is regulated through State rules and enforcement."},
{"no":63,"text":"Consider the following statements about India's External Debt:\n1. Most of India's external debt is owed by Government.\n2. All external debt is denominated in US Dollars.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both","D":"Neither","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Public Finance","tags":["External Debt","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"The source states that a significant portion of India's external debt belongs to commercial borrowers, rather than being entirely government debt.\nExternal debt is denominated in multiple currencies.\nTherefore, neither statement is correct.","revisionPoints":"External debt can be owed by:\nGovernment\nBanks\nCorporates\nOther entities\nIt can be denominated in different foreign currencies.\nCurrency composition affects exchange-rate risk.\nExternal debt differs from domestic debt."},
{"no":64,"text":"Which of the following is NOT an asset of a commercial bank?","A":"Advances","B":"Deposits","C":"Investments","D":"Money at call and short notice","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking","tags":["Bank Balance Sheet","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Deposits are money that customers place with the bank.\nFrom the bank's perspective, deposits represent an obligation/liability.\nAdvances and investments are assets because they generate income for the bank.\nMoney at call and short notice is also treated as an asset.","revisionPoints":"Bank assets → loans/advances, investments, cash/balances\nBank liabilities → deposits and borrowings\nDeposits are liabilities because banks owe the money to depositors.\nBank balance sheet questions are common in UPSC Economy."},
{"no":65,"text":"Which of the following reduce the risk of a currency crisis?\n1. Foreign earnings from IT sector\n2. Higher government expenditure\n3. Remittances","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 3 only","C":"2 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Financial Markets","tags":["Currency Crisis","Forex Reserves","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"IT services generate foreign exchange earnings.\nRemittances from Indians abroad provide another source of foreign exchange.\nHigher government expenditure by itself does not necessarily strengthen foreign-exchange reserves and can increase macroeconomic pressures depending on its financing and composition.","revisionPoints":"Foreign exchange sources include:\nMerchandise exports\nServices exports\nRemittances\nCapital inflows\nStrong foreign-exchange reserves can improve external-sector resilience.\nCurrency crisis can involve severe pressure on the exchange rate and foreign-exchange reserves."},
{"no":66,"text":"Which committee suggested that Governors should be eminent outsiders?","A":"First Administrative Reforms Commission","B":"Rajamannar Committee","C":"Sarkaria Commission","D":"National Commission to Review the Constitution","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Governor","tags":["Sarkaria Commission","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"The Sarkaria Commission examined Centre-State relations.\nIt made recommendations concerning the appointment and functioning of Governors.\nIt emphasized the importance of appointing individuals with appropriate stature and qualifications.","revisionPoints":"Sarkaria Commission → Centre-State relations\nConstituted in 1983.\nExamined:\nGovernor\nInter-State Council\nLegislative relations\nAdministrative relations\nGovernor-related recommendations are frequently tested."},
{"no":67,"text":"Which instrument enables foreign investors to invest without direct registration?","A":"Certificate of Deposit","B":"Commercial Paper","C":"Promissory Note","D":"Participatory Note","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Financial Markets","tags":["Participatory Notes","FPI","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Participatory Notes (P-Notes) are financial instruments issued by registered foreign portfolio investors.\nThey allow overseas investors to gain exposure to Indian securities without directly registering as investors in the same manner.","revisionPoints":"P-Notes → Participatory Notes\nAssociated with Foreign Portfolio Investment.\nIssued by registered foreign portfolio investors.\nThey have been subject to regulatory scrutiny concerning transparency and beneficial ownership."},
{"no":68,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding CAMPA:\n1. CAMPA exists at National and State levels.\n2. People's participation is mandatory.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both","D":"Neither","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Sustainable Development","tags":["CAMPA","Afforestation","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"CAMPA operates through national and state-level institutional structures.\nPeople's participation is not treated as a mandatory requirement in the source.\nCAMPA is associated with the management of funds relating to compensatory afforestation.","revisionPoints":"CAMPA → Compensatory Afforestation Fund Management and Planning Authority\nAssociated with compensatory afforestation.\nNational and State CAMPA structures exist.\nFunds are linked to diversion of forest land for non-forest purposes."},
{"no":69,"text":"Independent Regulators are reviewed by which of the following?\n1. Ad-hoc Committees\n2. Department Related Standing Committees\n3. Finance Commission\n4. Financial Sector Legislative Reforms Commission\n5. NITI Aayog","A":"1 and 2","B":"1, 3 and 4","C":"3, 4 and 5","D":"2 and 5","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Government Bodies","tags":["Regulatory Bodies","Parliamentary Committees","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Independent regulators can be examined through parliamentary committees.\nAd-hoc committees may also examine specific regulatory matters.\nFinance Commission and NITI Aayog do not constitute the general parliamentary review mechanism for independent regulators described here.","revisionPoints":"Parliamentary committees strengthen legislative oversight.\nDepartment-related Standing Committees examine the functioning of ministries and related subjects.\nRegulatory bodies are generally created through statutes or other legal frameworks.\nAccountability and independence must operate together."},
{"no":70,"text":"Consider the following statements about Five-Year Plans:\n1. Second Plan emphasized heavy industries.\n2. Fourth Plan aimed at correcting concentration of wealth.\n3. Fifth Plan first included financial sector.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Planning","tags":["Five-Year Plans","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"The Second Five-Year Plan focused strongly on heavy industries and industrialization.\nThe Fourth Plan emphasized growth along with objectives related to economic stability and reducing inequality.\nThe source considers statement 3 incorrect.","revisionPoints":"First Plan → Agriculture and irrigation\nSecond Plan → Mahalanobis strategy, heavy industry\nFourth Plan → Growth with stability\nFifth Plan → Poverty removal and self-reliance"},
{"no":71,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding the Asian Infrastructure Investment Bank (AIIB):\n1. AIIB has more than 80 members.\n2. India is the largest shareholder.\n3. AIIB has no members from outside Asia.","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"International Financial Institutions","tags":["AIIB","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"AIIB has more than 80 members.\nThe source states that China, not India, is the largest shareholder.\nAIIB includes members from outside Asia as well.","revisionPoints":"AIIB → Asian Infrastructure Investment Bank\nHeadquarters → Beijing\nChina is the largest shareholder.\nIndia is an important member and shareholder.\nMembership includes countries from both Asia and outside Asia."},
{"no":72,"text":"Inter-Creditor Agreement (ICA) is associated with","A":"Reduction of fiscal deficit","B":"Infrastructure lending","C":"Independent regulator","D":"Faster resolution of stressed assets","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking","tags":["Inter-Creditor Agreement","NPA Resolution","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"An Inter-Creditor Agreement provides a framework for coordination among creditors.\nIt can facilitate coordinated action when a borrower has stressed assets.\nThe objective is to make the resolution process more efficient.","revisionPoints":"ICA → coordination among creditors\nImportant in resolution of stressed assets/loans.\nHelps creditors coordinate decisions.\nLinked with efforts to address banking-sector NPAs."},
{"no":73,"text":"Chairman of Public Sector Banks is selected by","A":"Banks Board Bureau","B":"RBI","C":"Ministry of Finance","D":"Concerned Bank","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking","tags":["Banks Board Bureau","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"According to the source, the Banks Board Bureau (BBB) was responsible for recommending/selecting top-level appointments in public-sector banks.\nIt was created to improve professionalism in bank appointments.","revisionPoints":"Banks Board Bureau → public-sector bank appointments\nBBB was established to improve governance and professionalism.\nIt was later replaced by the Financial Services Institutions Bureau (FSIB).\nDistinguish historical institutional arrangements from current ones."},
{"no":74,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding PNGRB:\n1. It was the first regulatory body established by the Government.\n2. It ensures competitive gas markets.\n3. Appeals lie before the Appellate Tribunal for Electricity (APTEL).","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Energy Sector Regulation","tags":["PNGRB","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"PNGRB stands for Petroleum and Natural Gas Regulatory Board.\nIt promotes competitive markets in petroleum and natural gas-related activities.\nAppeals against its orders lie before APTEL.\nIt was not the first regulatory body established by the Government.","revisionPoints":"PNGRB → Petroleum and Natural Gas Regulatory Board\nRegulates downstream petroleum and natural gas activities.\nPromotes competition and orderly development.\nAppeals → APTEL."},
{"no":75,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding LTE and VoLTE:\n1. LTE is marketed as 3G and VoLTE as advanced 3G.\n2. LTE is data-only while VoLTE enables voice over LTE.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both","D":"Neither","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"IT & Computers","tags":["LTE","VoLTE","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"LTE is associated with 4G mobile communication technology, not 3G.\nVoLTE means Voice over LTE.\nVoLTE allows voice calls to be carried through an LTE network.","revisionPoints":"LTE → Long-Term Evolution\nLTE → 4G technology.\nVoLTE → Voice over LTE\nVoLTE uses the LTE packet-switched network for voice communication."},
{"no":76,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding the Maternity Benefit (Amendment) Act, 2017:\n1. Pregnant women are entitled to 3 months pre-delivery and 3 months post-delivery leave.\n2. Establishments with crèche facilities must allow six crèche visits per day.\n3. Women with two or more surviving children receive reduced maternity benefits.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Labour & Employment","tags":["Maternity Benefit Act","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"The amendment increased maternity leave to 26 weeks for eligible women in specified circumstances.\nUp to 8 weeks can be taken before the expected date of delivery.\nThe law provides for a crèche facility in establishments meeting the applicable conditions.\nWomen with two or more surviving children receive 12 weeks of maternity benefit.","revisionPoints":"Maternity Benefit Amendment Act → 2017\nStandard enhanced leave → 26 weeks for eligible women.\nFor women with two or more surviving children → 12 weeks.\nCrèche facility → eligible establishments; prescribed visits are provided for."},
{"no":77,"text":"Ease of Doing Business Index — Which is NOT a sub-index?","A":"Maintenance of law and order","B":"Paying Taxes","C":"Registering Property","D":"Dealing with Construction Permits","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Ease of Doing Business","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Paying Taxes, Registering Property and Dealing with Construction Permits were among the areas covered by the World Bank's historical Doing Business indicators.\nMaintenance of law and order was not one of the standard Doing Business indicators.","revisionPoints":"Historical Doing Business indicators covered areas such as:\nStarting a Business\nPaying Taxes\nRegistering Property\nGetting Credit\nConstruction Permits\nThe World Bank discontinued the Doing Business report in 2021.\nDo not treat the historical index as a current World Bank ranking."},
{"no":78,"text":"Extended Producer Responsibility (EPR) was introduced prominently under","A":"Biomedical Waste Rules","B":"Plastic Rules, 1999","C":"E-Waste (Management and Handling) Rules, 2011","D":"Food Safety Regulations","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Pollution","tags":["Extended Producer Responsibility","E-Waste Rules","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"The source associates the prominent introduction of Extended Producer Responsibility (EPR) with the E-Waste Rules, 2011.\nEPR places responsibility on producers for managing products and their waste during the post-consumer stage.","revisionPoints":"EPR → producer responsibility for post-consumer waste\nImportant in:\nE-waste\nPlastic waste\nBattery waste\nEncourages recycling, recovery and environmentally sound waste management."},
{"no":79,"text":"Economic cost of foodgrains is calculated as:\nEconomic Cost = MSP + Bonus + ?","A":"Transportation only","B":"Interest only","C":"Procurement incidentals + Distribution cost","D":"Procurement incidentals + Godown charges","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Economic Cost of Foodgrains","FCI","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Economic cost includes the cost of procuring foodgrains at the applicable MSP/bonus.\nIt also includes procurement incidentals.\nDistribution costs are added to arrive at the economic cost.","revisionPoints":"Economic cost of foodgrain → MSP + procurement-related costs + distribution cost\nProcurement incidentals can include handling and related expenses.\nFood Corporation of India plays a major role in procurement and distribution.\nEconomic cost is relevant to food subsidy calculations."},
{"no":80,"text":"Which of the following represents social capital?","A":"Literacy","B":"Buildings","C":"Working-age population","D":"Mutual trust and harmony","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Social Capital","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Social capital refers to networks, relationships, trust and norms that facilitate cooperation.\nMutual trust and social harmony can strengthen cooperation among individuals and groups.\nLiteracy is generally associated with human capital.\nBuildings are physical capital.","revisionPoints":"Social capital → trust, networks, cooperation and social relationships\nHuman capital → education, skills and health\nPhysical capital → machinery, buildings and infrastructure\nSocial capital can improve collective action and institutional functioning."},
{"no":81,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding the Constitution:\n1. High Courts cannot declare Central laws unconstitutional.\n2. Constitutional amendments can be reviewed by the Supreme Court.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both","D":"Neither","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Judiciary","tags":["Judicial Review","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"High Courts have the power of judicial review and can examine the constitutional validity of Central as well as State laws.\nTherefore, statement 1 is incorrect.\nConstitutional amendments can be subjected to judicial review, particularly if they violate the Basic Structure of the Constitution.","revisionPoints":"Judicial Review → Supreme Court + High Courts\nHigh Courts can examine Central and State legislation.\nBasic Structure Doctrine → Kesavananda Bharati case, 1973\nConstitutional amendments are not completely beyond judicial review."},
{"no":82,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding Purchasing Power Parity (PPP):\n1. PPP compares the same basket of goods.\n2. India is the sixth largest economy in PPP terms.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both","D":"Neither","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Purchasing Power Parity","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"PPP compares the purchasing power of currencies by considering the prices of a comparable basket of goods and services.\nThe source considers the statement about India's sixth position in PPP terms incorrect for the period/question context.","revisionPoints":"PPP → Purchasing Power Parity\nUsed to compare the purchasing power of different currencies.\nHelps compare economies using internationally comparable prices.\nDistinguish nominal GDP from GDP at PPP."},
{"no":83,"text":"Consider the following statements about Kharif crops:\n1. Rice area is highest.\n2. Jowar area is greater than oilseeds.\n3. Cotton area is greater than sugarcane.\n4. Sugarcane area has steadily decreased.","A":"1 and 3 only","B":"2, 3 and 4 only","C":"2 and 4 only","D":"All","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Kharif Crops","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Rice occupies a very large area among Kharif crops.\nThe source considers the statement about jowar having greater area than oilseeds incorrect.\nCotton has a larger cultivated area than sugarcane in the comparison used.\nSugarcane area has not shown the continuous decrease stated.","revisionPoints":"Kharif crops → mainly monsoon-season crops\nMajor Kharif crops:\nRice\nMaize\nCotton\nSoybean\nGroundnut\nRabi → wheat, gram, mustard etc.\nCrop area and production are different indicators."},
{"no":84,"text":"Which one of the following has the highest value among India's agricultural imports?","A":"Spices","B":"Fresh fruits","C":"Pulses","D":"Vegetable oils","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"International Trade","tags":["Agricultural Imports","Edible Oils","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Vegetable oils constitute a major component of India's agricultural import bill.\nIndia has significant domestic demand for edible oils.\nDomestic production has historically not been sufficient to meet total consumption.","revisionPoints":"India is heavily dependent on imports for edible oils.\nMajor edible oils include:\nPalm oil\nSoybean oil\nSunflower oil\nAgricultural trade depends on both domestic production and consumption demand."},
{"no":85,"text":"Which is the best definition of Liberty?","A":"Protection from rulers","B":"Absence of restraint","C":"Doing whatever one likes","D":"Opportunity to develop oneself fully","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Political Theory","tags":["Liberty","Political Theory","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Liberty is not simply the absence of every restriction.\nIn political philosophy, meaningful liberty also involves creating conditions that allow individuals to develop their capabilities and personality.\nTherefore, the source identifies opportunity for self-development as the best definition.","revisionPoints":"Negative liberty → absence of undue restraints\nPositive liberty → conditions enabling self-development\nLiberty is different from unrestricted freedom to do anything.\nConstitutional liberty operates within a framework of rights and reasonable restrictions."},
{"no":86,"text":"Which of the following will NOT stop Rupee depreciation?","A":"Reduce imports","B":"Masala Bonds","C":"Ease ECB norms","D":"Expansionary Monetary Policy","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Financial Markets","tags":["Rupee Depreciation","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Reducing imports can reduce demand for foreign currency.\nMasala Bonds can facilitate foreign-currency inflows into India through rupee-denominated bonds.\nEasing ECB norms can potentially increase foreign capital inflows.\nExpansionary monetary policy can reduce interest-rate attractiveness and may put downward pressure on the domestic currency.","revisionPoints":"Rupee depreciation → fall in value of rupee against another currency.\nFactors supporting rupee:\nExport earnings\nCapital inflows\nRemittances\nAdequate forex reserves\nMasala Bonds → rupee-denominated bonds issued overseas.\nMonetary policy affects exchange rates through interest rates and capital flows."},
{"no":87,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding RBI Data Localization:\n1. Payment data stored only in India.\n2. Public sector ownership is mandatory.\n3. Audit report must be submitted to CAG.","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking","tags":["Data Localization","RBI","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"RBI's payment-data localization framework requires relevant payment-system data to be stored in India.\nIt does not require the payment-system entity to have mandatory public-sector ownership.\nThe source does not support a requirement that such audit reports must be submitted to the CAG.","revisionPoints":"Data localization → storing specified data within a particular jurisdiction\nRBI introduced requirements concerning payment-system data.\nObjectives include:\nRegulatory access\nData security\nOversight\nData localization is different from data ownership."},
{"no":88,"text":"GDPR was implemented by","A":"Australia","B":"Canada","C":"European Union","D":"USA","ans":"C","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Organizations","tags":["GDPR","Data Protection","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"GDPR stands for General Data Protection Regulation.\nIt is the European Union's major data-protection and privacy framework.\nIt regulates the processing and protection of personal data.","revisionPoints":"GDPR → General Data Protection Regulation\nImplemented by the European Union.\nFocuses on:\nPersonal data protection\nPrivacy\nData processing\nRights of data subjects\nCame into force in 2018."},
{"no":89,"text":"Nuclear Cooperation Action Plan was signed by India with","A":"Japan","B":"Russia","C":"UK","D":"USA","ans":"D","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Defence Cooperation","tags":["Nuclear Cooperation","India-USA","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"The India-US civil nuclear relationship was developed through a series of agreements and arrangements.\nThe India-US Civil Nuclear Agreement was a major development in bilateral nuclear cooperation.\nThe source identifies the United States as the answer.","revisionPoints":"India–US Civil Nuclear Agreement → major strategic development\nAssociated with India's access to international civil nuclear cooperation.\nNSG waiver, 2008 was an important related development.\nNuclear cooperation involves issues of safeguards, fuel supply and peaceful nuclear use."},
{"no":90,"text":"Money Multiplier increases when","A":"CRR increases","B":"Banking habits increase","C":"SLR increases","D":"Population increases","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["Money Multiplier","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Greater use of banking channels can increase the proportion of money held within the banking system.\nHigher CRR or SLR generally reduces the funds available for lending and therefore tends to reduce the money multiplier.\nPopulation growth by itself does not determine the money multiplier.","revisionPoints":"Money multiplier → relationship between monetary base and money supply\nHigher reserve requirements generally reduce banks' lending capacity.\nCRR → Cash Reserve Ratio\nSLR → Statutory Liquidity Ratio\nBanking habits and deposit mobilization influence the process of credit creation."},
{"no":91,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding AR and VR:\n1. AR overlays digital content on the real world.\n2. VR provides complete immersion.","A":"1 and 2","B":"3 and 4","C":"1, 2 and 3","D":"4 only","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"IT & Computers","tags":["Augmented Reality","Virtual Reality","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"According to the source, the correct statements listed are 3 and 4, which describe the technologies as:\nAR → overlays digital information/content onto the real world\nVR → provides an immersive virtual environment\nThe numbering in the source excerpt begins with statements 3 and 4, so the answer follows the supplied source.","revisionPoints":"AR → Augmented Reality\nAdds digital elements to the user's real-world environment.\nVR → Virtual Reality\nCreates a computer-generated immersive environment.\nAR and VR are widely used in education, gaming, medicine, defence and training."},
{"no":92,"text":"Denisovan refers to","A":"Dinosaur","B":"Early human species","C":"Cave system","D":"Geological period","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Denisovan","Human Evolution","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Denisovans were an archaic human group identified through genetic and fossil evidence.\nThey are related to other ancient human populations such as Neanderthals and modern humans.","revisionPoints":"Denisovans → archaic humans\nEvidence came significantly from the Denisova Cave in Siberia.\nAncient DNA played an important role in identifying the population.\nDenisovan genetic ancestry is found in some present-day human populations."},
{"no":93,"text":"Which of the following is NOT correct regarding functional/artificial chromosomes?","A":"Functional chromosomes can be made using DNA from different species.","B":"Artificial DNA can be created.","C":"DNA from an animal cell can be inserted into an outside living cell.","D":"Plant/animal cells can be cultured in laboratories.","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biotechnology","tags":["Synthetic Biology","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Artificial chromosome and synthetic biology techniques involve manipulation and construction of genetic material.\nDNA sequences can be synthesized artificially.\nCells from plants and animals can be cultured under laboratory conditions.\nThe source identifies statement (c) as incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Synthetic biology → design/manipulation of biological systems\nDNA can be chemically synthesized.\nCell culture → growth of cells under controlled laboratory conditions\nGenetic engineering can involve transfer or modification of genetic material."},
{"no":94,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding Digital Signature:\n1. It identifies the Certifying Authority.\n2. It is proof of identity.\n3. It ensures document integrity.","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"IT & Computers","tags":["Digital Signature","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"A digital signature uses cryptographic techniques to verify that a document has not been altered after signing.\nThus, it provides data/document integrity.\nThe source does not treat the other statements as correct in the context of the question.","revisionPoints":"Digital signature → cryptographic authentication mechanism\nProvides:\nIntegrity\nAuthentication\nNon-repudiation in appropriate contexts\nCertifying Authorities issue digital signature certificates.\nDigital signature ≠ simply a scanned handwritten signature."},
{"no":95,"text":"Consider the following applications of Wearable Technology:\n1. Location tracking\n2. Sleep monitoring\n3. Hearing assistance","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"IT & Computers","tags":["Wearable Technology","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Wearable devices can use GPS and other sensors for location tracking.\nSmartwatches and fitness devices can monitor sleep patterns.\nSpecialized wearable devices can provide hearing assistance.\nTherefore, all three are possible applications.","revisionPoints":"Wearables can contain:\nGPS\nAccelerometers\nHeart-rate sensors\nTemperature sensors\nApplications include health monitoring, navigation, fitness and communication.\nWearable technology is part of the broader Internet of Things (IoT) ecosystem."},
{"no":96,"text":"RNA Interference (RNAi) has applications in:\n1. Gene silencing\n2. Cancer therapy\n3. Hormone replacement\n4. Virus-resistant crops","A":"1, 2 and 4","B":"2 and 3","C":"1 and 3","D":"1 and 4","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biotechnology","tags":["RNA Interference","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"RNA interference is a biological mechanism that can silence specific gene expression.\nIt has been investigated for therapeutic applications, including cancer research.\nRNAi technology can also be used to develop virus-resistant crops.\nHormone replacement is not a standard RNAi application.","revisionPoints":"RNAi → gene silencing\nInvolves small RNA molecules such as siRNA and miRNA.\nApplications:\nDisease research\nTherapeutics\nCrop improvement\nPest/disease resistance\nRNAi works by interfering with gene expression."},
{"no":97,"text":"Black Hole Merger discovery was confirmed through","A":"Higgs Boson","B":"Gravitational Waves","C":"Wormholes","D":"Singularity","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Space","tags":["Gravitational Waves","Black Holes","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"The merger of black holes produces powerful gravitational waves.\nThese ripples in spacetime were directly detected by observatories such as LIGO.\nThe detection provided direct evidence of gravitational waves from such cosmic events.","revisionPoints":"Gravitational waves → ripples in spacetime\nPredicted by Albert Einstein's General Relativity.\nLIGO → Laser Interferometer Gravitational-Wave Observatory\nFirst direct detection of gravitational waves was announced in 2016, from a 2015 event."},
{"no":98,"text":"Consider the following as causes of Multi-drug Resistance:\n1. Genetic predisposition\n2. Wrong antibiotic dosage\n3. Antibiotic use in livestock\n4. Multiple chronic diseases","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 and 3","C":"1, 3 and 4","D":"2, 3 and 4","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biotechnology","tags":["Antimicrobial Resistance","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Incorrect or inappropriate antibiotic use can promote antimicrobial resistance.\nUse of antibiotics in livestock can also contribute to selection and spread of resistant organisms.\nThe source identifies statements 2 and 3 as the relevant causes in this question.","revisionPoints":"Antimicrobial resistance (AMR) → microorganisms become resistant to antimicrobial drugs\nMajor drivers:\nInappropriate antibiotic use\nOveruse\nIncomplete/incorrect treatment\nAgricultural use\nAMR can make infections harder to treat.\nIt is a major global public-health concern."},
{"no":99,"text":"Cas9 Protein is","A":"Molecular scissors for gene editing","B":"Biosensor","C":"Pest resistance gene","D":"Herbicide","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biotechnology","tags":["CRISPR","Cas9","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Cas9 is an enzyme associated with the CRISPR-Cas9 gene-editing system.\nGuided by RNA, Cas9 can cut DNA at a targeted location.\nThis enables scientists to modify specific genetic sequences.","revisionPoints":"CRISPR-Cas9 → gene-editing technology\nCas9 → DNA-cutting enzyme\nGuide RNA directs Cas9 to the target DNA sequence.\nApplications include:\nGenetic research\nDisease studies\nPotential therapies\nCrop improvement"},
{"no":100,"text":"Which statement is NOT correct?","A":"Hepatitis B spreads much like HIV.","B":"Hepatitis B has vaccine; Hepatitis C does not.","C":"Hepatitis B infections globally are much higher than HIV.","D":"Hepatitis B & C may remain asymptomatic for years.","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Hepatitis","UPSC","2019"],"expl":"Hepatitis B has an effective vaccine.\nThere is currently no approved vaccine for Hepatitis C.\nHepatitis B and C can both cause chronic infection.\nChronic infection can remain asymptomatic for years before complications become apparent.","revisionPoints":"Hepatitis B → vaccine available\nHepatitis C → no approved vaccine\nBoth can cause chronic liver disease.\nChronic hepatitis can lead to:\nCirrhosis\nLiver failure\nLiver cancer\nHepatitis B and C differ in their routes of transmission and prevention strategies."},
];
